  "worked_solution": "The Lagrange error bound states that an upper bound for the error ${R}_{3}$ obtained when using the third-degree Taylor polynomial ${P}_{3}$ to approximate $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{M}{4!}|x-1{|}^{4},\n$$\n\nwhere $M$ is the maximum value of $|{f}^{(4)}(x)|$ on the interval between $x=0.65$ and $a=1.$ \n\nPlugging $x=0.65$ into the above formula gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{3}| & \\le \\frac{M}{4!}|0.65-1{|}^{4} \\\\ |{R}_{3}| & \\le \\frac{M}{24}|-0.35{|}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $M,$ we need to compute $|{f}^{(4)}(x)|$ and then maximize it on the interval $[0.65,1].$ Taking the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ |{f}^{(4)}(x)| & =|{e}^{x}|={e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $|{f}^{(4)}(x)|={e}^{x}$ is an increasing function on the interval $[0.65,1],$ its value is greatest at $x=1.$ So\n\n$$\nM=|{f}^{(4)}(1)|={e}^{1}=e,\n$$\n\nand therefore, the upper bound is\n\n$$\n|{R}_{3}|\\le \\frac{e}{24}|-0.35{|}^{4}≈0.001\\,7,\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places.",
